faq:
  - q: "What are the most important sections of a LinkedIn profile?"
    a: "In order of recruiter search impact: (1) Headline — most searched field after your name. (2) Current job title field — how LinkedIn categorizes you in search results. (3) Skills section and endorsements — directly weighted in search ranking. (4) About section — where you establish identity and value. (5) Experience descriptions — keyword-rich bullets improve keyword density and recruiter read-through. Most candidates optimize 1–2 of these and leave the rest generic."
  - q: "How long does it take to optimize a LinkedIn profile?"
    a: "The actual writing and editing takes 3–6 hours for a full optimization. The majority of time goes to the headline, About section, and experience bullets. The mechanical sections (skills, education, contact info) take 30–45 minutes. The return: sustained recruiter inbound for months after the update."
  - q: "Does LinkedIn profile picture matter?"
    a: "Yes. Profiles with professional photos receive 21x more profile views and 36x more messages than those without. The photo does not need to be professionally shot — it needs to be professional in appearance: clear, recent, business-appropriate background, direct eye contact. No group photos, no vacation photos, no low-resolution crops."
  - q: "How do I know if my LinkedIn profile is optimized?"
    a: "Check four signals: (1) All-Star profile status (visible in profile strength meter). (2) Weekly profile views — optimized profiles in active searches typically see 50–200+ views per week. (3) Search Appearances data (shows which keywords you appeared for and how many times). (4) Recruiter InMail volume — if you are getting fewer than 2–3 InMails per week in an active job market, the profile needs optimization."
  - q: "What is LinkedIn All-Star status?"
    a: "All-Star is LinkedIn's highest profile completeness rating. It requires: profile photo, industry and location, current position with description, education, at least 5 skills, and 50+ connections. Profiles with All-Star status appear higher in LinkedIn search results. It is the floor, not the ceiling — All-Star is necessary but not sufficient for strong recruiter visibility."
  - q: "Should I put Open to Work on my LinkedIn profile?"
    a: "Use the Open to Work feature in recruiter-only mode — visible to recruiters but not your current employer or their network. Set specific target job titles (not 'open to anything'), preferred locations, and start date. The green 'Open to Work' banner visible to all is optional — it increases recruiter outreach but is visible to your current employer."
howto:
  name: "How to Optimize Your LinkedIn Profile"
  steps:
    - name: "Write a keyword-rich headline"
      text: "Your headline is the most important section for recruiter search visibility. Use the formula: [Level + Title] | [Specialization] | [Value or targeting signal]. LinkedIn gives you 220 characters — use at least 150. Include your exact target role title and 2–3 domain keywords."
    - name: "Write a strong About section"
      text: "Four parts: identity opening (level + role + domain), outcomes (2–3 quantified results), specialization (the specific problems you solve best), and a targeting statement. First line must establish your professional identity before 'see more.' Write in first person."
    - name: "Optimize your Experience section"
      text: "Each role should have 3–5 quantified bullet points. Use your job title field accurately — this is how LinkedIn categorizes you in search. Include relevant keywords in your experience descriptions, not just in your Skills section. Quantify every outcome you can."
    - name: "Fill your Skills section to capacity"
      text: "LinkedIn allows up to 50 skills — fill it completely. Prioritize skills from your target job descriptions (exact matches, not synonyms). Get endorsements for the top 5–10 most important skills — endorsed skills carry more weight in search ranking."
    - name: "Turn on Open to Work in recruiter-only mode"
      text: "Specify exact target job titles (not 'open to opportunities'). Add preferred locations, work arrangements (remote/hybrid/onsite), and your start date availability. The more specific, the more relevant the recruiter outreach."
    - name: "Add Featured content"
      text: "The Featured section (links, posts, media) appears prominently above your About section. Link to your portfolio, published work, GitHub, or a specific project. Profiles with Featured sections receive 2x more profile views than those without."
    - name: "Request 3–5 recommendations"
      text: "Prioritize recommendations from former managers. Ask them to focus on a specific project and include a quantified outcome. Provide a draft or bullet points if they seem uncertain. Three strong, specific recommendations are worth more than ten generic ones."
    - name: "Complete all remaining sections"
      text: "Education, certifications, volunteer work, languages, and publications all contribute to All-Star status and keyword density. Every section you complete improves your search ranking and profile credibility."
